# PDF to HTML conversion

When transforming a PDF into an HTML page in this project, follow these rules.

## 1. Content changes: spelling and grammar only

- **Only** fix spelling and grammar in the extracted text.
- Do **not** change structure, meaning, or wording beyond corrections.
- Preserve paragraphs, lists, and logical sections when building the HTML.
- Respect project rules: anonymization (200_anonymization-legal.mdc), privacy (306_privacy-personal-data.mdc).

## 2. Language and filenames

- **If the PDF content is in French:**
  - Keep the French text (with spelling/grammar fixes) in an HTML page whose filename ends with **`-fr`** (e.g. `document-name-fr.html`).
  - **Also** create an **English translated version** of the same page (e.g. `document-name.html` or `document-name-en.html`). Prefer the same base name without `-fr` for the English version when there is only one language pair.
- **If the PDF content is in another language** (e.g. English only): produce a single HTML page with spelling/grammar fixes; no `-fr` suffix. Add an English version only if an official or existing French version exists elsewhere and you are mirroring that pattern.

## 3. HTML structure and consistency

- Use the same page chrome as the rest of the site: theme toggle, Google Translate widget, print button, nav (back to home), main content, footer. See **120_page-chrome-consistency.mdc**.
- Set `<html lang="fr">` for French pages and `<html lang="en">` for English pages.
- Reuse existing CSS and layout patterns from `public/` (e.g. `demande-explications-autorites-competentes.html`, `people-contacted.html`).
- Add the new URLs to `public/sitemap.xml` and use canonical links where appropriate.

## 4. Naming summary

| Source language | French HTML file      | English HTML file   |
|-----------------|-----------------------|----------------------|
| French          | `something-fr.html`   | `something.html` or `something-en.html` |
| English only    | —                     | `something.html`    |

When in doubt, prefer one French file (`-fr`) and one English file (no suffix or `-en`) per document.
